  "doc.appendix.toolsP": "The seven tools fall into three groups; tool names must be referenced exactly and are case-sensitive. A disabled tool does not appear in the Agent's tool list. See chapter 08 for parameters.",
  "doc.appendix.toolsTitle": "Tool names at a glance",
  "doc.audit.debugTitle": "Troubleshooting with the audit log",
  "doc.audit.detailP1": "Open any row to see the full token, tool, connection, status, elapsed time and row count; denials and errors also show a reason, and the SQL section holds the copyable statement.",
  "doc.audit.detailP2": "SQL text is stored up to 20,000 characters, after which it is truncated and marked; reasons are capped at 500 characters. Keep the full SQL elsewhere if you need it verbatim.",
  "doc.audit.detailTitle": "Details: what really happened",
  "doc.audit.fieldsP": "A record contains: time, token, tool, connection, status, reason, elapsed time, returned rows and SQL text. The SQL is the version that actually ran (after policy rewriting), so you can see how the row limit was applied.",
  "doc.audit.lead": "Once an Agent has your database, the first question is what it actually did. The audit log records every tool call in time order: who started it, which database it touched, what SQL ran, and how it ended.",
  "doc.audit.listP1": "The list shows six columns newest first: time, token, tool, connection, status and details. The details column shows a short reason or \"{rows} rows / {elapsed}ms\", so failed or empty calls stand out.",
  "doc.audit.listP2": "Filter in the top-right by start date, end date, tool, connection, status and token, in any combination; Clear filters restores everything. The list shows 50 rows per page and loads at most 5000 records at a time — narrow the range when you need more.",
  "doc.audit.listTitle": "List and filters",
  "doc.audit.recordP": "Every tool call is written to local SQLite: successes, policy denials, timeouts, truncated results and execution errors. System actions such as starting the service are recorded too, with the source shown as \"System\". If the audit write fails, the call itself fails — there is no \"executed but unlogged\" path.",
  "doc.audit.recordTitle": "What gets recorded",
  "doc.audit.redactP1": "SQL literal redaction is off by default, so statements are stored as written. When enabled, string and numeric literals are replaced with <code>REDACTED</code>, while structure, table and function names stay, so the statement type is still clear.",
  "doc.audit.redactP2": "With redaction on, statements that fail to parse are stored as <code>[SQL REDACTED: PARSE FAILED]</code> and unknown dialects as <code>[SQL REDACTED: DIALECT UNKNOWN]</code>; it never falls back to plaintext.",
  "doc.audit.redactTitle": "Redaction and retention",
  "doc.audit.redactWarnText": "With redaction off, string constants, ID numbers, phone numbers and anything else in the SQL are written to <code>audit.db</code> verbatim. For production data, enable Redact SQL literals and never upload the audit file to a public location.",
  "doc.audit.redactWarnTitle": "Logs can contain business data",
  "doc.audit.retentionP1": "Retention defaults to 7 days, with 3, 7, 15 and 30 as options. Each new record also deletes records older than the cutoff, so the log cannot grow forever; shortening retention does not bring deleted history back.",
  "doc.audit.retentionP2": "Clear logs on the audit page wipes every record, usually after a handover or a debugging session; make sure you no longer need the evidence. The audit database sits next to the config, so back up more than <code>config.toml</code>.",
  "doc.audit.sourceBreaker": "Automatic circuit breaker",
  "doc.audit.sourceBreakerReason": "The token hit the denial threshold inside the window and was disabled automatically.",
  "doc.audit.sourceBreakerWhere": "Re-enable the token under Access control, or adjust the window and threshold.",
  "doc.audit.sourcePolicy": "Read-only policy",
  "doc.audit.sourcePolicyReason": "Policy messages about DDL, DML, multiple statements, side-effect functions or parse failures.",
  "doc.audit.sourcePolicyWhere": "Rewrite the SQL; pre-check it under Settings → General → Policy console.",
  "doc.audit.sourceToken": "Token permissions",
  "doc.audit.sourceTokenReason": "\"not allowed for this access token\" or \"tool is disabled\".",
  "doc.audit.sourceTokenWhere": "Adjust the token under Access control, or enable the tool on the Tools page.",
  "doc.audit.sourcesP": "A Denied status can come from three very different places. The reason text tells them apart, and each needs a different fix.",
  "doc.audit.sourcesP2": "Starting the service, auto-starting MCP and the breaker action itself also leave system records, shown as \"System\" in the token column. When a token suddenly stops working, find the circuit-breaker record first, then look at the denials before it.",
  "doc.audit.sourcesTitle": "Three sources of denial",
  "doc.audit.statusAllowed": "Allowed",
  "doc.audit.statusAllowedDesc": "The policy allowed the statement and the query succeeded.",
  "doc.audit.statusDenied": "Denied",
  "doc.audit.statusDeniedDesc": "Blocked by the read-only policy, token permissions or the circuit breaker; nothing ran.",
  "doc.audit.statusError": "Error",
  "doc.audit.statusErrorDesc": "The connection, SQL, driver or database returned an error; the reason keeps the original message.",
  "doc.audit.statusTimeout": "Timeout",
  "doc.audit.statusTimeoutDesc": "The connection's query deadline was exceeded and the connection was dropped.",
  "doc.audit.statusTruncated": "Truncated",
  "doc.audit.statusTruncatedDesc": "The query succeeded, but the result exceeded the row or byte limit; the data returned is incomplete.",
  "doc.audit.step1p": "Choose Denied, Timeout or Error to narrow to failed calls, then use the date range to locate the session.",
  "doc.audit.step1t": "Filter by status",
  "doc.audit.step2p": "The reason separates policy, permission, connection and database errors. Follow it to the right chapter instead of reinstalling or raising limits.",
  "doc.audit.step2t": "Read the reason",
  "doc.audit.step3p": "Copy the SQL from details and replay it in the Policy console with the same dialect; you will see the rewritten row limit and whether multiple statements or side effects were involved.",
  "doc.audit.step3t": "Inspect SQL and policy",
  "doc.audit.step4p": "If one token or connection fails while others work, the problem is permissions or that connection; if every token fails, check the service and the network.",
  "doc.audit.step4t": "Compare tokens and connections",
  "doc.audit.thMeaning": "Meaning",
